Let me analyze this PHP code carefully.

```php
$a = [1, 2, 3];
foreach ($a as &$v) {}
$v = 99;
echo implode(',', $a);
```

This is the classic PHP foreach by reference bug.

1. `$a = [1, 2, 3];` — array is [1, 2, 3]
2. `foreach ($a as &$v) {}` — iterates through the array by reference. After the loop ends, `$v` is still a reference to the last element of `$a`, which is `$a[2]` (value 3).
3. `$v = 99;` — since `$v` is still a reference to `$a[2]`, this sets `$a[2]` to 99. Now `$a = [1, 2, 99]`.
4. `echo implode(',', $a);` — outputs "1,2,99"

The answer is "1,2,99".

